ixels":317,"insar_mm_yr":-2.08},{"id":"ZSE1","gps_mm_yr":-1.58,"gps_sigma":0.46,"used":false,"years":"2003-2025","pixels":293,"insar_mm_yr":-1.56}],"gps_summary":"One constant ties the radar to the 4 GPS stations inside the map that have a well-determined rate. After that, radar and GPS vertical rates differ by 0.2 mm a year (root mean square). With so few stations that figure flatters the radar, so every rate's uncertainty uses the GPS stations' own uncertainty instead, 0.5 mm a year. Counting all 26 stations, including those outside the map or with a very uncertain GPS rate (marked *), the difference is 1.2 mm a year. Some GPS records ended before the radar record began in 2016, so part of the difference is the two instruments measuring different years."},"nisar":{"ok":true,"summary":"NISAR's L-band radar passed over Seattle 10 times between June 2026 and October 2026. Adding its 9 consecutive interferograms gives the ground motion over those 120 days. In so short a time the long-term rates predict that neighborhoods should differ from each other by only about 0.3 mm. NISAR's neighborhood values scatter by about 5 mm. That scatter is the atmosphere and ionosphere delaying the radar signal, not the ground moving, so NISAR cannot yet confirm or contradict the long-term rates. It needs a year or more of passes before the noise averages down to millimeters. In a typical 12-day pair NISAR returned a measurement over 80% of the land in these neighborhoods, and the Sentinel-1 record gives a reliable long-term rate over 78%. Each figure uses its own product's quality flags, so the two are about level here: in a built-up city both radars see most of the ground.","product":"NISAR L2 GUNW (provisional)","doi":"10.5067/NIL2GUNW-P1","track_frame":"106_A_026","band":"L","polarization":"HH","first_date":"20260621","last_date":"20261019","days":120,"n_pairs":9,"neighborhood_scatter_mm":4.5,"expected_range_mm":0.33,"correlation":0.03,"land_measured_typical_pair":0.803,"land_measur